“### Atoms

Single-byte machine-code ops (`tools/mkblob2.py` `ATOMS`): the compiler copies each one's template inline.

| byte | glyph | mnemonic | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
“‹tl_z›←‹atoms›′‹tl_rrowd›‹tl_each›
“
### Compiler ops

Single-byte tokens the compiler handles itself (prefixes, delimiters, definition headers) — there is no template behind them.

| byte | glyph | mnemonic | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
“‹tl_z›←‹comps›′‹tl_rrowd›‹tl_each›
“
### Library words

Words defined by the prelude (`lib/prelude.golfj`). A letter word is called by its ASCII letter; its glyph and mnemonic are input aliases for that letter. A high-byte word has no letter.

| byte | glyph | mnemonic | word | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|---|
“‹tl_z›←‹libs›′‹tl_rrowl›‹tl_each›
“
### Digraph page

Two-byte words: `ʘ` (`\dig`, `0xBD`) then any byte B compiles as a call to the long name `‹_dgHH›` (HH = B in upper-case hex). The prelude gives a word a digraph with `:‹_dgHH›‹name›;`. Generated from `lib/prelude.golfj`.

| digraph | bytes | long name | word | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|---|
“‹tl_z›
‹dict›→‹dg›“lib/prelude.golfj“⊃‹readfile›10‹split›′‹tl_dgline›‹tl_each›
←‹dg›‹keys›⍋⇒k 0⇒i{⇐i⇐k≢<1+⋄⇐k⇐i⊇⇒b⇡i←‹dg›⇐b‹get›⇒r
 “| `“‹tl_z›⇐b‹tl_dgglyph›‹tl_md›“` | `BD “‹tl_z›⇐b‹tl_hx2›‹tl_s›“` | `‹_dg“‹tl_z›⇐b‹tl_hx2›‹tl_s›
 “›` | `“‹tl_z›⇐r 0⊇‹tl_md›“` | “‹tl_z›⇐r 1⊇‹tl_md›“ |
“‹tl_z›}
⇐k≢[“| — | | | | none defined yet |
“‹tl_z›];
